# RedLab Cursor AI Rules (v1.1.1)

# Context & Product
- Project: RedLab (Visual Network Attack Simulation & Cyber Defense Laboratory)
- Core Concept: Cisco Packet Tracer meets Kali Linux for educational cybersecurity.
- Stack: React 19 + TypeScript + Vite + Zustand + React Flow (Frontend) | Python 3.14 + FastAPI + SQLAlchemy + SQLite (Backend)
- Theme: Dark Cybersecurity Laboratory (#0B0D12, #11151D, #171C26, #293140, #FF3B4E). Never use bright or light themes.

# Strict Architecture Guidelines
1. Zero Cloud Services: Strictly computer network security (TCP/IP, ICMP, ARP, firewalls, ports, subnets, routers, web servers). DO NOT introduce AWS, S3, IAM, or cloud SDKs.
2. State: Use Zustand (`useEditorStore` and `useSimulationStore`). Never put shared lab or topology state in local component state.
3. Canvas: Visual nodes/edges must adhere to `@xyflow/react` conventions and serialize cleanly to `GraphData` (nodes/edges).
4. Deterministic Simulation: Attack executions and security rules must evaluate real topology graphs in `backend/app/network_engine/` and `backend/app/security_engine/`. Never hardcode mock findings or fake scans.
5. Educational Forensics: Port scans and attacks must explain who closed ports (Kernel TCP RST vs Firewall DROP) and remediation steps.
6. Schemas: Keep `backend/app/schemas/lab.py` and `frontend/src/types/index.ts` 100% synchronized.
7. Simplified Beginner UI: Keep toolbar clean; use prominent Attack/Defend mode toggle and interactive Linux terminal.

# Git Branching
- Never commit directly to `main` or `develop`.
- Work only in designated feature branches:
  - `feature/canvas-*` (Canvas, cables, custom nodes)
  - `feature/ui-*` (Inspector forms, modals, dashboard)
  - `feature/security-*` (Network attack scenarios, rules, heuristics)
  - `feature/backend-*` (FastAPI endpoints, DB, import/export)
  - `feature/qa-*` (Tests & CI pipelines)
- Follow conventional commits: `feat:`, `fix:`, `test:`, `refactor:`, `docs:`.

# Testing Mandate
- Frontend: Run `npm run build` and `npm test` inside `frontend/` before declaring work done.
- Backend: Run `python3 -m pytest tests/ -v` inside `backend/` before declaring work done.
